## 6. Context-Aware and Multimodal Personalization

The next wave of personalization integrates multiple contextual signals—device orientation, ambient light, geolocation, and even calendar events—into UI adaptation. By combining these contextual inputs with traditional behavioral data, interfaces achieve a deeper understanding of the user’s real‐world circumstances.

For example, mobile UIs can adjust contrast and font size when sensors detect outdoor sunlight or a high‐motion environment, improving accessibility and legibility. At the same time, notifications can be suppressed during calendar‐indicated focus periods or automatically routed to “do not disturb” modes when driving is detected via GPS and accelerometer data.

Moreover, multimodal personalization marries visual, auditory, and haptic channels for a seamless experience. Voice assistants now incorporate visual cues—like dynamic on‐screen suggestions—tailored to the user’s browsing history, while haptic feedback intensifies for high‐priority alerts. This orchestrated approach ensures each modality works in concert, delivering messages in the medium and mode most likely to capture user attention and be contextually appropriate.

